After clinching the league title in dramatic fashion the previous season,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Clark |first=Graham |date=5 May 1986 |title=The Ecstasy and the Agony |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=nOJAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=0KYMAAAAIBAJ&pg=6652%2C1231979 |url-status=live |work=[[Glasgow Times]] |pages=26-27}}</ref> Celtic entered the 1986–87 campaign as defending champions. It marked manager [[David Hay|Davie Hay]]’s first league championship since his appointment in the summer of 1983, and this coming season would be his fourth season in charge. The club's objectives for the season included retaining the league title and making progress on their return to the [[1986–87 European Cup|European Cup]].

== Season Overview ==
There were no major additions to the first-team squad during the summer. Three young players were recruited: Northern Irish full-back [[Anton Rogan]], plus fellow countryman and goalkeeper [[Allen McKnight]] were signed from [[Lisburn Distillery F.C.|Lisburn Distillery]], then winger [[Mark Smith (footballer, born 1964)|Mark Smith]], was signed from [[Queen's Park F.C.|Queen's Park]]. As the season progressed, the lack of significant investment in the playing squad became a source of tension between Hay and the club's board of directors.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Reynolds |first=Jim |date=26 November 1986 |title=Celtic 1m transfer speculation "rubbish" |url=https://news.google.co.uk/newspapers?id=B6VAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=paUMAAAAIBAJ&pg=4176%2C6960413 |work=[[The Herald (Glasgow)]] |pages=30}}</ref> Plus media speculation about the futures of star strikers [[Mo Johnston]] and [[Brian McClair]] increased as the season progressed.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Quinn |first=John |date=31 March 1987 |title=Hay slams McClair link |url=https://books.google.es/books?id=pMlAAAAAIBAJ&lpg=PA19&dq=davie%20hay%20mcclair&pg=PA19#v=onepage&q=davie%20hay%20mcclair&f=false |url-status=live |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=36}}</ref>

Celtic enjoyed a fairly successful pre-season, which included a tour of Ireland and two victories against English First Division opposition. These results generated optimism ahead of the competitive campaign.

=== August ===
The season began with Celtic unfurling the league flag at home, followed by a victory over Dundee. This was succeeded by two away wins against [[Motherwell F.C.|Motherwell]] and [[Clydebank F.C. (1965)|Clydebank]], maintaining an unbeaten start in the league.

The [[1986–87 Scottish League Cup|League Cup]] campaign (now known as the Skol Cup for sponsorship reasons) also commenced in August. Celtic recorded home victories against [[Airdrieonians F.C. (1878)|Airdrieonians]] and [[Dumbarton F.C.|Dumbarton]] in the second and third rounds respectively. While comfortable, the performances were not entirely convincing but set up a quarter-final tie against [[Aberdeen F.C.|Aberdeen]] in September.

The remaining league fixtures in August included a 1–1 draw away to title rivals [[Aberdeen F.C.|Aberdeen]], a match that underlined why both clubs were widely regarded as the strongest teams in the division. The following weekend the faced Glasgow rivals [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]], who hadn't won the league championship since [[1977–78 Scottish Premier Division|1978]], but had heavily invested in their squad since [[Graeme Souness]] arrived in April 1986, with England Internationals [[Chris Woods]] and [[Terry Butcher]] being the main attractions brought in over the summer. The first old firm test of the season saw Rangers win 1-0 comfortably outplaying Celtic for most of the game.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Reynolds |first=Jim |date=1 September 1986 |title=Durrant drives Rangers to victory |url=https://news.google.co.uk/newspapers?id=E7ZAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=qqUMAAAAIBAJ&pg=3566%2C79119 |url-status=live |work=[[The Herald (Glasgow)]] |pages=7}}</ref>

=== September ===
Following defeat in the Old Firm derby, Celtic responded positively in September with a strong run of form. In league competition, the team recorded three wins and a draw, re-establishing momentum in the title race. Celtic also progressed in the [[Skol Cup]], advancing through both the quarter-final and semi-final stages. Victories over [[Aberdeen F.C.|Aberdeen]] and [[Motherwell F.C.|Motherwell]] were secured via penalty shoot-outs, setting up a final against city rivals [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]]. In terms of transfers, manager [[Davie Hay]] confirmed that striker [[Alan McInally]] was not available for transfer<ref>{{Cite news |last=Quinn |first=John |date=9 September 1986 |title=Celtic hold on to Alan |url=https://books.google.es/books?id=W-xAAAAAIBAJ&lpg=PA18&dq=davie%20hay%20transfer&pg=PA18#v=onepage&q=davie%20hay%20transfer&f=false |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=31}}</ref> and would remain at the club as cover for first-choice forwards [[Mo Johnston]] and [[Brian McClair]]. During September Celtic Chairman Thomas Devlin passed away at the age of 72 after a long illness, he was appointed Chairman in June 1985 after the sudden death of long serving Chairman Desmond White. Devlin had been a director of Celtic since 1949 where he succeeded his father Thomas L. Devlin Snr, who briefly served as a director from 1940 until his death in 1941.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Quinn |first=John |date=18 September 1986 |title=Celtic chief Devlin is dead |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=YexAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=5qYMAAAAIBAJ&pg=5148%2C3709713 |url-status=live |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=17}}</ref>

=== October ===
Celtic continued their strong league form by winning all their league fixtures during the month of October, claiming five wins out of five. The 26th October Celtic faced Rangers in the [[1986 Scottish League Cup final|Skol League Cup final]] at [[Hampden Park]], a controversial match ensued with [[Mo Johnston]] being red carded for Celtic before [[Tony Shepherd]] was also sent off but refused to leave the field. [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]] clinched the first trophy of the [[1986–87 in Scottish football|domestic season]] thanks to a late [[Davie Cooper]] penalty awarded after much protest from Celtic.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Reynolds |first=Jim |date=27 October 1986 |title=Ten booked, one sent off in Rangers' triumph |url=https://news.google.co.uk/newspapers?id=HIJDAAAAIBAJ&sjid=q6UMAAAAIBAJ&pg=4019%2C6600937 |work=[[The Herald (Glasgow)]] |pages=9}}</ref> 

In the [[1986–87 European Cup|European Cup]], Celtic progressed comfortably through the first round, defeating [[Shamrock Rovers F.C.|Shamrock Rovers]] 3–0 on aggregate, although performances were widely regarded as unconvincing.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://news.google.co.uk/newspapers?id=CYJDAAAAIBAJ&sjid=q6UMAAAAIBAJ&pg=2498%2C493568|title=Johnston at the double as Celtic go through easily|last=Reynolds|first=Jim|date=2 October 1986|pages=20|url-status=live|work=[[The Herald (Glasgow)]]}}</ref> This victory set up a challenging second-round tie against [[Soviet Top League|Soviet]] champions and reigning [[1986 European Cup Winners' Cup final|European Cup Winners’ Cup]] holders [[FC Dynamo Kyiv|Dinamo Kiev]]. Celtic were eliminated after a 4–2 aggregate defeat to the Soviet side.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Davidson |first=Alan |date=6 November 1986 |title=Celtic's nearly men |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=BuBAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=8aYMAAAAIBAJ&pg=6408%2C1407318 |work=[[The Glasgow Timesl]] |pages=42-43}}</ref>

=== November ===
Celtic kept up their league form in November, winning all of their fixtures except for a draw away to [[Aberdeen F.C.|Aberdeen]]. However, performances declined in December, with the team managing only one victory, alongside three draws and a defeat to Hearts. Despite the downturn in results, Celtic entered the New Year still leading the league by five points, although Rangers had a game in hand. The Old Firm derby at the turn of the year proved pivotal, as Rangers delivered a composed performance against a poor Celtic side to secure a 2–0 victory. The result significantly dented Celtic's title challenge while providing momentum for Rangers.

Celtic would drop further points during the January and February, many citing that although Celtic had a potent strike force, their poor defense cost them points. [[David Hay|Davie Hay]]'s efforts to strengthen the team fell on deaf ears from the board led by new Chairman Jack McGinn.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Reynolds |first=Jim |date=26 November 1986 |title=Celtic 1m transfer speculation "rubbish" |url=https://books.google.es/books?id=B6VAAAAAIBAJ&lpg=PA30&dq=davie%20hay%20jack%20mcginn&pg=PA30#v=onepage&q=davie%20hay%20jack%20mcginn&f=false |work=[[The Herald (Glasgow)]]}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite news |last=Paul |first=Ian |date=2 March 1987 |title=Angry Hay confronts his players with brutal truth |url=https://news.google.co.uk/newspapers?id=cC41AAAAIBAJ&sjid=_KULAAAAIBAJ&pg=2737%2C103044 |work=[[The Herald (Glasgow)]] |pages=9}}</ref> In February [[David Hay|Davie Hay]] appointed [[Tommy Craig]] as his assistant manager, the role had been vacant since the controversial sacking of [[Frank Connor]] during the previous season.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Reynolds |first=Jim |date=3 February 1986 |title=Celtic sack Connor |url=https://news.google.co.uk/newspapers?id=0bdAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=y6UMAAAAIBAJ&pg=5804%2C371079 |work=[[The Herald (Glasgow)]] |pages=9}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Paul |first=Ian |date=20 February 1987 |title=Craig is No. 2 at Parkhead |url=https://news.google.co.uk/newspapers?id=UvQ9AAAAIBAJ&sjid=AEkMAAAAIBAJ&pg=1595%2C4945347 |work=[[The Herald (Glasgow)]] |pages=30}}</ref> There was not much cheer either in the [[1986–87 Scottish Cup|Scottish Cup]], a third round victory over [[Aberdeen F.C.|Aberdeen]] after two replays was followed by a fourth round defeat to [[Heart of Midlothian F.C.|Hearts]] who ended Celtic's hopes of any cup silverware for the season. 

The only trophy left was the League title but a 4–1 defeat away to [[Dundee F.C.|Dundee]] at the end of February allowed [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]] to claim top spot.<ref name=":0" /> Another defeat away at Pittodrie at the hands of Aberdeen saw Celtic's title challenge falter even further. Although Celtic did regain some form in March and April including a win against Rangers at [[Celtic Park]],<ref>{{Cite news |last=Reynolds |first=Jim |date=6 April 1987 |title=Celtic hustle Rangers out of their title stride |url=https://news.google.co.uk/newspapers?id=SRA1AAAAIBAJ&sjid=sKULAAAAIBAJ&pg=2246%2C1219128 |url-status=live |work=[[The Herald (Glasgow)]] |pages=12}}</ref> further points dropped against [[Dundee United F.C.|Dundee Utd]] and Rangers hitting form, meant the league title would go to Ibrox for the first time in nine years and Celtic would end the season without a trophy. A poor defeat to [[Falkirk F.C.|Falkirk]] at home on the second last weekend of the season would seal the fate of the title.

Celtic would go trophyless into their centenary season, with speculation around several players futures, especially Celtic's top two strikers [[Mo Johnston]] and [[Brian McClair]]. There also was talk about [[David Hay|Davie Hay]]'s suitability to continue to lead the team.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Paul |first=Ian |date=4 May 1987 |title=McGinn picks up the Ibrox gauntlet |url=https://news.google.co.uk/newspapers?id=_Y1DAAAAIBAJ&sjid=OFkMAAAAIBAJ&pg=6772%2C794923 |url-status=live |work=[[The Herald (Glasgow)]] |pages=7}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Quinn |first=John |date=14 May 1987 |title=McClair says "no" |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=bAk-AAAAIBAJ&sjid=zUkMAAAAIBAJ&pg=6077%2C2582729 |url-status=live |work=[[The Glasgow Times]] |pages=44}}</ref>

== Pre-season and friendlies ==
Celtic prepared for the 1986–87 with a summer tour of [[Republic of Ireland|Ireland]] in late July, playing 3 games including [[Packie Bonner|Pat Bonner]]'s twin brother Denis testimonial game in Galway. The then followed up with a home friendly against Aston Villa and then finished up pre season preparation by travelling to London to play in David O'Leary's testimonial match at Highbury.{{football box collapsible|id=Longford Town v Celtic|date=27 July 1986|time=19:00 GMT|round=[[Exhibition game#Club football|Friendly]]|score=1-1|report=https://www.thecelticwiki.com/1986-07-27-to-07-31-pre-season-ireland/|team1=[[Longford Town]] {{flagicon|IRL}}|goals1=|team2={{flagicon|SCO}} Celtic|goals2=[[Owen Archdeacon|Archdeacon]] {{goal|7}}|stadium=Rugby Ground|location=[[Longford]], Republic of Ireland|attendance=10,000|referee=|result=D}}{{football box collapsible|id=Galway United F.C. v Celtic|date=29 July 1986|time=19:00 GMT|round=[[Exhibition game#Club football|Denis Bonner Testimonial]]|score=0-2|report=https://www.thecelticwiki.com/1986-07-27-to-07-31-pre-season-ireland/|team1=[[Galway United F.C.]] {{flagicon|IRL}}|goals1=|team2={{flagicon|SCO}} Celtic|goals2=|stadium=|location=[[Galway]], Republic of Ireland|attendance=|referee=|result=W}}{{football box collapsible|id=Cobh Ramblers F.C. v Celtic|date=31 July 1986|time=19:15 GMT|round=[[Exhibition game#Club football|Friendly]]|score=1–4|report=[https://www.thecelticwiki.com/1987-07-23-tidaholms-gif-1-2-celtic-friendly/ Report]|team1=Cobh Ramblers F.C. {{flagicon|IRL}}|goals1=|team2={{flagicon|SCO}} Celtic|goals2=|stadium=St Colman's Park|location=[[Cobh]], Republic of Ireland|attendance=|referee=|result=W}}{{football box collapsible|id=Celtic v Aston Villa|date=2 August 1986|time=15:00 GMT|round=[[Exhibition game#Club football|Friendly]]|score=1–0|report=https://news.google.com/newspapers?nid=GGgVawPscysC&dat=19860804&printsec=frontpage&hl=en|team1=Celtic {{flagicon|SCO}}|goals1=[[Brian McClair|McClair]] {{goal|24}}|team2={{flagicon|ENG}} [[Aston Villa F.C.]]|goals2=|stadium=[[Celtic Park]]|location=[[Glasgow]], Scotland|attendance=|referee=[[Kenny Hope]] (Clarkston)|result=W}}{{football box collapsible|id=Arsenal v Celtic|date=5 August 1986|time=|round=[[Exhibition game#Club football|David O'Leary Testimonial]]|score=0–2|report=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=Z7ZAAAAAIBAJ&sjid=sKUMAAAAIBAJ&pg=6365%2C1521335|team1=[[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] {{flagicon|ENG}}|goals1=|team2={{flagicon|SCO}} Celtic|goals2=[[Mo Johnston|Johnston]] {{goal|80}}, [[Alan McInally|McInally]] {{goal|85}}|stadium=[[Arsenal Stadium|Highbury]]|location=[[London]], England|attendance=30,000|referee=|result=W}}

